cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its analysis outputs, please cite it as below."
title: "Prevalence, Thresholds, and Subgroup Performance Gaps in Clinical Prediction Models: A Multi-Dataset Analysis"
type: software
repository-code: "https://github.com/Zavashgh/clinical-prediction-subgroup-gaps"
version: "v1.1.0"
authors:
  - family-names: Ghorbanpourbaboli
    given-names: Zavash
  - family-names: Afshar
    given-names: Arina
  - family-names: Jangravi
    given-names: Elham
  - family-names: Alaoui Mhamdi
    given-names: Mohammed Ayoub
  - family-names: Butler
    given-names: Russell
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Analysis code, held-out predictions, and reference outputs for a retrospective
  secondary analysis of five publicly available clinical prediction datasets,
  examining within-dataset subgroup sensitivity differences across sex, age, and
  race/ethnicity, and how prevalence standardization and operating thresholds
  affect their interpretation. All data-derived preprocessing is fitted inside
  each cross-validation and calibration fold on that fold's training
  observations only. The Diabetes-130 dataset is partitioned, cross-validated,
  calibrated, and resampled at the patient level, with uncertainty from a joint
  patient-cluster bootstrap.
keywords:
  - clinical prediction models
  - machine learning
  - algorithmic bias
  - health equity
  - model evaluation
  - model calibration
